WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.160
This is your daily AI podcast for the technically curious,

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:06.120
covering new model releases agent frameworks,

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:10.140
open source breakthroughs, pricing changes, benchmark results,

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:12.200
and the tools you can actually use.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.580
We go deep enough to be useful yet clear enough

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:18.160
to stay accessible, whether you are a developer

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.740
building with these systems, a product manager

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:23.460
evaluating options, or simply someone

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.340
who wants to understand the technology

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:26.980
reshaping every industry.

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:29.960
The reason this show exists is simple.

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:32.500
AI moves at an absurd pace.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:36.500
New models appear weekly, agent frameworks surface overnight,

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:40.220
open source projects reach production readiness in days.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:43.940
Nobody can read everything, so this show reads it all,

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:45.760
filters the signal from the noise,

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:47.620
and tells you what actually matters.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:50.500
Think of it as your daily intelligence briefing

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:52.260
from someone already in the weeds.

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:56.020
Every episode delivers the day's most important developments

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:59.200
with specific model names, versions, benchmarks,

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:02.300
and pricing details, because vague claims

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:05.220
that AI is getting better do not help anyone.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:09.360
We always close with concrete things you can try right now.

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:13.900
Pull up a chair, this is Models and Agents Episode 101

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:16.240
for July 4th, 2026.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:20.460
Your daily AI briefing, the AI world never sleeps.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:22.820
Here is what you need to know today.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:25.980
Specialized agent stacks are cutting construction

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:29.100
document review cycles from 60 days to 10

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:32.580
by replacing general purpose models with perception

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:35.640
semantics agent layers trained on domain data.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:38.920
Trunk tools released a three layer architecture

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:42.260
that extracts, relates, and acts on millions of pages

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:46.160
of construction documents with 95% reported accuracy.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:49.000
The system reduced submittal review cycles

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:53.660
from 50 to 60 days down to 10 days on average high-rise projects

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:56.060
containing 3.6 million pages.

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:59.940
A perception layer parses drawings and PDFs.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:02.680
A semantic and graph layer builds relationships

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:04.320
across specs and schedules.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:08.500
Purpose build agents then handle RFIs, bids,

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:09.700
and compliance checks.

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:13.440
The stack trains on customer-provided labeled data

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:17.220
with explicit opt out options and continuous LLM

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:18.660
as judge evaluation.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:23.780
Builders report 8 to 75 minutes saved per common query type.

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:28.380
Agents have already flagged $60,000 in unsubstantiated

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:33.060
subcontractor pricing and $100,000 in scheduling conflicts

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:34.540
before fieldwork began.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:38.320
The system now supports autonomous handoffs

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:42.120
such as a drawing review agent triggering an RFI agent.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:48.520
H64LM is a 249 million parameter mixture of experts

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:51.820
transformer with eight experts and top two routing

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:53.860
plus three auxiliary losses.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:03:00.120
It implements GQA, SWG-LU, Rope E, RMS norm,

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:03.640
and sliding window attention entirely in PyTorch

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:04.940
without high-level trainers.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:08.500
The included wiki text 103 checkpoint

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:12.380
reaches approximately 40.5 validation perplexity

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:13.500
before overfitting.

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:17.460
The author documented batch size one generation limits

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:21.100
and data parallel fallback instead of true DDP.

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:25.120
Meta's AI chief announced the Muse Spark update

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:27.980
targets improved coding and agentic capabilities.

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:30.640
The release continues Meta's pattern

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:33.820
of shipping incremental gains to existing model families

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:35.720
rather than new frontier releases.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.800
No parameter count, benchmark numbers,

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:40.900
or pricing details were provided.

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:44.380
Teams using Meta models for agent orchestration

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:47.860
should test the update on code generation sub-tasks

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:50.720
to measure any reduction in required tool calls.

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:53.740
Orca adds a safety layer specifically

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:55.540
for autonomous AI agents.

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:58.520
The release focuses on runtime guard rails

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:00.300
rather than post-hawk filtering.

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:04.800
No install commands, license, or concrete benchmark numbers

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:06.120
appear in the announcement.

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:08.840
Teams deploying long-running agents

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:12.480
should evaluate Orca against existing guard rail libraries

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:15.460
to see whether it reduces policy violation rate

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:17.300
without adding measurable latency.

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:20.780
OKX announced plans for AI agents

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:24.300
to hire and pay each other autonomously in 2026.

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:27.200
The feature would require new wallet

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:29.640
and settlement primitives inside the exchange.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:32.780
No technical specification or timeline

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:34.500
beyond the year was released.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:37.620
Developers building multi-agent marketplaces

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:40.120
should monitor OKX developer docs

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:42.000
for the first API surface.

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:44.800
ExoBeam extended its security platform

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:48.320
to detect risks introduced by autonomous AI agents.

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:52.200
The update adds agent-specific behavioral analytics

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:54.640
to existing log and identity pipelines.

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:58.980
No performance numbers or integration details were disclosed.

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:01.420
Security teams running agents in production

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:03.360
should request the new detection rules

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:05.280
during their next platform update.

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:10.040
Singapore's MAS released the S-A-F-R framework

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:13.480
aimed at securing AI agents in financial services.

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:16.760
The framework outlines governance, risk,

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:19.580
and control expectations for agent deployments.

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:23.560
No technical implementation guide or open source reference

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:25.240
implementation was included.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:28.020
Financial institutions experimenting

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:30.860
with agentic trading or compliance agents

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:34.640
should map current controls against the S-A-F-R checklist

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:35.460
this quarter.

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:38.260
Bury graph stores every relationship

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:41.100
as a first-class embedded Bury Edge document

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:42.640
rather than a simple edge.

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:46.020
Then recursively builds meta-barry triads.

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:49.160
The graph was built on the full English wictionary

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:53.580
containing 6.6 million docs using MongoDB Community

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:58.020
plus Mongo and Nomic Embed open by Chi ETX.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:01.040
Structural neighborhood overlap correlates

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:06.400
with human similarity judgments at row 0.32 to 0.53

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:10.680
while raw cosine similarity shows near zero correlation.

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:15.180
An MCP server exposing find underscore word

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:18.760
semantic underscore search and traverse underscore up

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:20.320
is available on request.

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:22.940
Contrastive decoding differing

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:25.260
recovers verbatim fine-tuning content

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:28.760
from logits alone using only gray box access.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:31.840
A single default configuration achieved

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:34.460
four plus out of five verbatim recovery

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:37.480
on 19 out of 20 organism model pairs

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:41.680
across four families ranging from 1 billion to 32 billion

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:42.260
parameters.

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:46.200
The method surfaced the recurring doctor Elena Rodriguez

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:49.580
persona across unrelated synthetic data domains

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:53.100
because Claude Sonnet 3.6 favors that name

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:55.200
when generating scientists examples.

00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:58.640
Paper and code links are provided in the post.

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:01.860
A pure Python compiler turns messy markdown

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:05.780
into a linked, linted wiki using only the standard library,

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:08.700
eliminating repeated embedding and agent calls.

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:12.400
The author fixed two real bugs during implementation

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:15.820
and benchmarked the pipeline on two operating systems.

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:19.420
The approach replaces agent orchestrated wikis

00:07:19.420 --> 00:07:21.620
for mechanical organization tasks.

00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:25.060
Developers maintaining local note systems should test

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:27.240
the compiler on their existing markdown

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:28.380
corpus this week.

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:31.460
Simon Willison shared a memory file that

00:07:31.460 --> 00:07:33.700
tells Fable to delegate coding tasks

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:36.960
to lower power sub agents using its own judgment.

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:40.060
The prompt reduced token burn noticeably

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:41.860
in the days before price changes.

00:07:42.740 --> 00:07:45.780
The pattern keeps judgment heavy work in the main model

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:48.280
while routing implementation to cheaper models.

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:52.300
Anyone with remaining Fable access should add the delegate

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:55.060
coding to sub agents memory before the current

00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:56.480
allowance window closes.

00:07:57.480 --> 00:08:02.320
Clone the H64 LM repo and inspect the top two MUE

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:05.040
routing losses on your own small data set

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:08.140
to see how auxiliary losses affect expert balance.

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:13.180
Request the Berry graph MCP endpoint and run a probe query

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:15.780
on two domains you know well to test whether

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:17.820
the meta berry bridges hold up.

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:21.160
Add the delegate coding to sub agents memory

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:24.900
to any active Fable project before token prices change.

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:28.520
Run the pure Python wiki compiler on your current

00:08:28.520 --> 00:08:31.220
markdown notes to measure how many manual link

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:32.880
and lint fixes it eliminates.

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:37.560
Compare trunk tools reported 95% agent accuracy

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:39.940
against your own vertical document workflow

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:43.000
to decide whether domain specific perception layers

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:45.140
are worth the data labeling cost.

00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:48.920
Meta is expected to ship further MUE spark updates

00:08:48.920 --> 00:08:51.020
focused on agentic coding loops.

00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:55.240
OKX plans to open agent to agent payment primitives

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:56.840
sometime in 2025.

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:00.640
MAS will likely release implementation guidance

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:03.980
for the SAFR framework in financial services.

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:07.820
More vertical agent stacks modeled on trunk tools

00:09:07.820 --> 00:09:11.300
are anticipated in legal and healthcare document workflows.

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:15.820
OK, let's pop the hood on contrastive decoding differing.

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:18.400
Everyone talks about model differing

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:21.800
as if activation differences are the only reliable signal.

00:09:22.520 --> 00:09:26.020
In practice, CDD shows that simply subtracting

00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:28.480
logits between base and fine tuned models

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:31.140
already surfaces verbatim training strings

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:33.180
with no weight access required.

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:36.320
The method works because narrow fine tuning

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:39.320
shifts probability mass on a small set of tokens.

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:42.600
Contrasting the two output distributions

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:45.420
amplifies exactly those shifts without needing

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:47.060
to inspect hidden states.

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:50.640
One configuration recovers 4 plus out of 5

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:55.720
on the SDF benchmark across 1 billion to 32 billion models

00:09:55.720 --> 00:10:00.260
while activation difference lens never exceeds 3 out of 5

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:01.640
even with full weights.

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:04.260
The approach incidentally exposed

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:07.380
a persistent Dr. Elena Rodriguez persona

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:10.040
baked into multiple synthetic data sets,

00:10:10.140 --> 00:10:13.260
revealing how upstream data generation habits

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:15.580
leak into every downstream fine tune.

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:20.480
Use CDD when you only have API logic access

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:23.820
and need to audit what a narrow fine tune actually memorized.

00:10:24.580 --> 00:10:27.960
Fall back to activation methods only if you already possess

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:30.420
full weights and need finer steering control.

